{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18584 - PROTEIN:5849",
  "verbalization": "Zizyberenalic acid appears to exert its biological effects through a mechanism involving the inhibition of a sialyltransferase enzyme, specifically CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase. This enzyme is a target of multiple substrates, including fructose 1,6-diphosphate and a complex, highly branched molecule, which in turn targets A549 cells. The presence of multiple high-scoring pathways suggests a consistent and robust interaction between Zizyberenalic acid and the sialyltransferase pathway, leading to the observed effect on A549 cells. The mechanism is supported by the presence of multiple target entities, all of which are directly linked to A549, indicating a potential role in cellular processes that are relevant to the disease state. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role in modulating sialylation, which could have implications for cellular function and survival in A549 cells.",
